  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 923 AM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nAfter a cloudy, dry Friday morning and afternoon, another Pacific\nshortwave will commence the next round of showers and \nthunderstorms from the west in the evening. The showers and \nthunderstorms could produce occasional periods of heavy rainfall \nand flash flooding, especially in Luna, Grant, and Hidalgo \nCounties in New Mexico. Showers and thunderstorms will continue \nwell into the overnight hours until sunrise on All Hallow's Eve.\n\nFor what would be a Halloween Area Forecast Discussion, if the \nforecast was not a trick or a treat? Well, Halloween is expected \nto be a treat weather-wise, as a tiny shortwave ridge in between \nthese shortwave troughs will bring dry air for prime trick-or- \ntreating hours on Saturday. Skies will be partly cloudy and \ntemperatures in the upper 60s/low 70s on Halloween.\n\nAs November begins and the clocks turn back an hour, the upper-\nlevel trough and cold front will sweep through on Sunday. Showers\nand thunderstorms will develop west of Deming in the afternoon at\na quick pace thanks to healthy mid-level winds, speeding through \nthe region late Sunday night and into Monday morning. Showers\nshould move out by midday on Monday, and a drying trend begins \nthrough about next Friday. Temperatures early next week will be at\nor slightly below normal.",
